<!--
  /layout — the page that did not exist. <row>/<column>/<grid> are used on
  eighteen pages of this site and were taught on none of them.
-->
<column class="doc" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">What's given</p>
  <h1>Layout</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Five keywords, and <strong>the axis is the name</strong>. There is no
    <code>flex-direction</code> to get backwards, no debate about whether a stack
    is vertical, and no CSS to write.
  </p>

  <code-block code={code.axes}></code-block>

  <h2>Why this is a keyword and not a style</h2>
  <p>
    Flexbox is four decisions before you have laid anything out: which axis, how
    the main axis distributes, how the cross axis aligns, whether it wraps. Every
    one is a place two authors diverge — and an agent that isn't sure will guess
    <code>align-items</code> when it meant <code>justify-content</code>, produce
    something that looks nearly right, and ship it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So <code>sx</code> has no <code>flex-direction</code>, no
    <code>align-items</code>, no <code>grid-template-columns</code>. Not a gap —
    a refusal. Structure is a <em>keyword</em>, and the keyword names the axis.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.notSx}></code-block>

  <h2><code>&lt;row&gt;</code> — the x axis</h2>
  <p>
    <code>gap</code> · <code>align</code> (cross axis) · <code>justify</code>
    (main axis).
  </p>
  <row gap="3" align="center" justify="between" sx="p:4 bg:muted radius:md">
    <strong>Ada Lovelace</strong>
    <badge tone="success">Active</badge>
  </row>
  <code-block code={code.row}></code-block>

  <h2><code>&lt;column&gt;</code> — the y axis</h2>
  <p><code>gap</code> · <code>align</code>.</p>
  <code-block code={code.column}></code-block>

  <h2><code>&lt;grid&gt;</code> — two dimensions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>cols</code> for a fixed count; <code>min</code> for a responsive track
    that fills as many columns of at least that width as will fit. You do not
    write a media query.
  </p>
  <grid cols="3" gap="3">
    <card sx="p:4"><strong>One</strong></card>
    <card sx="p:4"><strong>Two</strong></card>
    <card sx="p:4"><strong>Three</strong></card>
  </grid>
  <code-block code={code.grid}></code-block>

  <h2><code>&lt;stack&gt;</code> — the z axis</h2>
  <p>
    Children occupy the same cell, one over another. For a badge on an avatar, an
    overlay on an image. It is the third axis, and it has a name for the same
    reason the other two do.
  </p>

  <h2><code>&lt;center&gt;</code></h2>
  <p>
    Centres its child on both axes. The single most-Googled question in CSS is a
    keyword here.
  </p>

  <h2>That is the whole vocabulary</h2>
  <p>
    Rows of columns of rows. Every layout you have ever built is those three,
    nested — and because they are keywords, two agents nest them the same way.
  </p>
  <code-block code={code.nesting}></code-block>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/elements">← Elements</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/styling">Styling →</a>
  </row>
</column>
